I used to work at CERN and I still go  out and work there a lot so I work on  their data and I work on mathematical  models of particle physics and how and  forces and the early universe and my  area of work is really between the data  and theory trying to inform one by the  other and sort of back things back and  forwards and up until last year I spent  all my career work working on the  supersymmetry theory which is a theory  of particle physics and but we thought  that the Large Hadron Collider  experiment at CERN was going to produce  particles predicted new particles  predicted by the supersymmetry theory  and it hasn't there's this hope hasn't  panned out so I've kind of shifted what  what I'm working on but the  supersymmetry theory was beautiful to  work on for a while it was it had very  lofty ideals it it was it was a bridge  to theories of everything theory like  mathematical theories of all particles  and forces which tried to explain  everything about our universe so you  know dark matter how forces work what  what women made of you know what light  is everything but you the problem with  them was you had to make quite a lot of  assumptions to to predict just a few  things that you could measure and test  in an experiment and then we looked in  the experiment and they weren't there so  yeah so I had to rethink last year and  now I'm working in a different mode I'm  looking at the data and looking for  glitches and so we predict with the  standard model of particle physics  that's the current knowledge that we  have we predict how the data should look  from the collisions between two protons  at CERN at high energies and we look for  cases where the the model disagrees the  prediction does  agrees with what's what scene and then  for that that for me is an opportunity  so then we we jump on that and try and  explain it but explain it in a very  simple way we kind of just bung in an  extra force or an extra particle that  would explain the what scene and then we  do some proper phenomenology so we try  and suggest other ways in which it may  be tested we first of all checked that  it's consistent with all other data and  then suggest other ways you can save the  data to look for these new particles or  forces and then you know go one step  further in the theoretical direction so  this is more we call it bottom-up  science working from the data and where  you've got really solid foundation and  you think you know what's going on and  then making hypotheses away from that so  I'm enjoying this new way of working  it's certainly quite different to the  previous way where you try and solve  everything  and make loads of assumptions that's  what that we called top-down you know  it's like you start from pure thought  thought and then try and get to your  experimental observations but for me  anyway that that that approach hasn't  panned out so you know I'm trying a new  approach and it's and it's a lot of fun  so this change in starting from a data  rather than theory does this mean that  you've sort of given up hope of finding  supersymmetrical particle right okay so  it's the work still focused on  supersymmetry are you that's just a  belief you have but you can't like no I  okay so looking at my own work at the  moment the way the way I answer this  question is the way for me to make  progress right now is not to work on  supersymmetry and the reason is because  we know from the data that the Large  Hadron Collider has seen already that in  the next few years there's not going to  be a big discovery of super  supersymmetric particles otherwise we  would have seen small fluctuations in  the data already so in the next years  for me that's not whether discovery is  going to be so I'm looking elsewhere but  still you know you can't help in your  heart of hearts  believe in this beautiful theory which  explains a really important fact that we  otherwise don't understand and that fact  is why is the Higgs boson so light so if  you if you do calculations on Higgs  bosons and they're they're affected by  quantum fluctuations the little the  seething of space-time in terms of other  particles and they're they're special in  that they feel those fluctuations  extremely sensitively and if you do  back-of-the-envelope calculations those  fluctuations should increase its mass by  something like a billion billion times  more than it then it's been measured to  be so this this is a puzzle that we  don't understand with our current theory  it's not explained why is the Higgs  boson so light and how is it able to  stay so light with these quantum  fluctuations supersymmetry explains this  it doesn't mean it's the only that  doesn't mean it's the only explanation  but the other ones don't work or you  know we haven't really found a decent of  the one so that's why we were so keen on  it and why we thought we might be able  to find supersymmetric particles as a  signal that the theory was right at the  Large Hadron Collider so you know if in  I don't know five or ten years time some  signals start appearing from the data of  course I'm going to jump back right back  on it right but for the moment I think  in terms of progress it's it's more  fruitful for me personally to look  elsewhere for more debates talks and  interviews subscribe today to the  Institute of Arts and ideas at IAI TV  you
 

